Michael Jackson&#x2019;s &#x201C;Thriller&#x201D; sets the tone for &#x201C; Shaun the Sheep: The Beast of Mossy Bottom ,&#x201D; the third feature film in Aardman Animations&#x2019; popular stop-motion franchise that follows Shaun as a mad scientist after the farmer ruins the flock&#x2019;s pumpkin patch the night before Halloween.

While the film opens with an upbeat homage to the King of Pop, bringing the sequence to life required elaborate staging. During Variety &#x2019;s Screening Series presented by Laika, &#x201C;Shaun the Sheep&#x201D; filmmakers Steve Cox and Matthew Walker broke down the elaborate stop-motion animation process and how they brought the beloved character into the monster-filled world of Halloween. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Sanaa Lathan and Terence Blanchard Join Sundance Winner ‘Jazz Infernal’ as Executive Producers (EXCLUSIVE) Moshe Rosenthal on Directing Queer Oscar Contender 'Tell Me Everything': 'Certain Films Open a Door in Your Heart for Your Own Memories'

The filmmakers initially considered opening with a time jump to a cargo ship at sea, where the monster breaks out of its hold. They ultimately abandoned the idea, however, as it was &#x201C;a bit too intense, and also too expensive,&#x201D; Walker told Variety senior entertainment writer Angelique Jackson.

&#x201C;It was scary in two different ways,&#x201D; Cox joked.

Their backup plan &#x2014; the &#x201C;Thriller&#x201D; homage &#x2014; began with the filmmakers positioning the puppets on set and staging run-throughs, which could take up to several days. Animator Chris Sadler then posed the characters in sections, creating a &#x201C;blocky&#x201D; version of the animation to match the soundtrack. From there, the team could make adjustments before moving into the more detailed parts of the stop-motion process.

Typically, the puppets&#x2019; feet are nailed into the floor to keep them steady during filming, but because the characters needed to dance, the team had to specially rig them with rods that could later be removed for the remaining scenes. The staging also proved too cramped to accommodate the entire flock in front of the barn, requiring the sequence to be filmed in three separate passes. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

But &#x201C;Thriller&#x201D; wasn&#x2019;t the only pop-culture reference featured in the film. &#x201C;Shaun the Sheep&#x201D; famously features minimal dialogue, but there was one word that caught the audience's attention: &#x201C;Hodor,&#x201D; the fan-favorite stableboy from &#x201C;Game of Thrones.&#x201D;

&#x201C;I just laughed out loud when I read it [in the script], because I was a big &#x2018;Game of Thrones&#x2019; fan,&#x201D; Cox recalled, laughing.

&#x201C;Shaun the Sheep: The Beast of Mossy Bottom&#x201D; initially began as a half-hour Valentine&#x2019;s Day special, centered on The Farmer meeting The Vet (a new character in the &#x201C;Shauniverse&#x201D;) and their budding relationship. As the creators explored the story further, however, they began gravitating toward Halloween, experimenting with different storylines and color palettes that would give the franchise a new look.

&#x201C;We really wanted to go bold with the colors and play around with the horror genre as well,&#x201D; Walker said. &#x201C;We&#x2019;re really conscious of not making it too scary, because obviously the audience for 'Shaun the Sheep' starts very young. Right from the beginning we were talking about it as a Halloween film, not a horror film, and just using the tropes of horror for comedy rather than scares.&#x201D;

Even as the story evolved, the filmmakers wanted to preserve The Farmer and The Vet&#x2019;s growing romance, plus introducing The Vet created room to explore a more emotional side of the franchise's characters.

&#x201C;We really wanted The Vet to feel like a character who could hopefully &#x2014; I'd love to see her again in future Shaun the Sheep projects,&#x201D; Walker says. &#x201C;So we spent a lot of time getting her character right, because we really loved the idea of her character.&#x201D;

The Vet also serves as something of an unwitting antagonist, with the flock initially frightened by her due to her occupation. While she shares a strong connection with The Farmer, the two have contrasting personalities: The Farmer is &#x201C;lazy and slow,&#x201D; while The Vet is &#x201C;energetic and proactive.&#x201D; But they find common ground in that they&#x2019;re shy around other humans.

One thing that also sets The Farmer apart is his transformation into a hairy beast. Developing the monster was an extensive design process, first drawing various sketches, then building clay sculptures created by the puppet department. The team experimented with different shapes, explored computer-generated versions with varying hairstyles and even created a prototype using layers of teddy bear fur. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#x201C;It took a long time to find the right look,&#x201D; Walker said. &#x201C;When we started, we knew it had to be mistaken for Bigfoot. That kind of influenced the design, and it's still The Farmer underneath, so it's meant to just be hair. &#x201D;

The filmmakers wanted the beast to have a significant amount of bulk and a distinct silhouette that audiences could immediately recognize. Its horn-shaped head was ultimately inspired by The Farmer&#x2019;s own receding hairline.

By the time the creative process was complete, the puppet of the beast featured a detailed Marquette inside and a 3D-printed core with control points that allowed animators to manipulate its movements. Its head was also mounted on a winder so it could move up and down.

In total, the puppet department created 11 different puppets of the character for the film.

&#x201C;With the beast, he's got such distinctive fronds of hair that come out of his head that each one of these things have to look identical,&#x201D; Cox said. &#x201C;So you have to give a big shout out to the puppet guys, that they've managed to make these puppets and make them look identical so that the continuity flows through the film.&#x201D;

The beast first appears as he climbs over the top of the mountain. The sequence was initially much scarier, the filmmakers revealed, with heavy footsteps that shook the camera and more intense music. But they ultimately decided to break up the tension with a switch in music and the comedic shot of the flock running into the house.

Shaun also gets a reimagined look in the film, complete with a lab coat, goggles, mad scientist hair and even a human glove with five fingers despite his distinctly shaped hooves. The costume was initially intended to be worn throughout the film, but the team ultimately reserved it for the moments when Shaun is plotting in his windmill laboratory, which was inspired by &#x201C;Frankenstein,&#x201D; Walker revealed.

Alongside the windmill, the filmmakers also built a large-scale ship that pushed the limits of both the production&#x2019;s budget and ambitions. The ship, the filmmakers said, was the largest single prop ever built for a &#x201C;Shaun the Sheep&#x201D; project.

&#x201C;We knew we had a limited budget at the start of the film, but Matt and I wanted to be really ambitious. We were pushing things as much as we could,&#x201D; Cox said. &#x201C;We took inspiration from James Cameron. He only built half the Titanic, so I was like, &#x2018;Well, we'll do the same as James Cameron. Let's do that!&#x2019;&#x201D; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

But in the shot of the ship leaving the dock, the unfinished side of the vessel began to show, which posed a challenge for Cox and Walker. In just three days, the creative team constructed the other half the ship to use for the shot.

The giant jack-o&#x2019;-lantern was also another ambitious moment in the film. Created through 3D printing, the pumpkin was so large that it exceeded the capabilities of the printers at Aardman. The team ultimately had to outsource the job to another company to bring the massive prop to life.

